## Step 1: Get the silhouette right

The input decides the result more than any parameter does. Check the picture before you write code.

**Best input:** a PNG (or WebP) with a transparent background, the subject whole (not cropped by the frame), long side 512–1024 px. If the user gives you one of these, skip to step 2.

**An SVG logo** works as it is: silkify rasterises it to 1024 px on its long side before inflating it (`image: 'logo.svg'`, a `data:image/svg+xml` URL, or an SVG `Blob`).

**A word, a headline, a letter or an emoji** needs no picture and no cut-out: pass the text and a font, `silkify(canvas, { text: 'silk', font: '800 200px Georgia, serif', color: '#6b2737' })` (`\n` for more lines). Use a heavy weight (700–900): the dome's height grows with the stroke's width, so hairlines barely inflate. Give the canvas the words' proportions (`aspect-ratio: 16 / 10` for a short word, wider for a line): the words fit inside it like a picture. End the stack with a generic family (`serif`, `sans-serif`): a system font missing on the visitor's machine then falls back quietly, as in CSS. A web font needs its `<link>` or `@font-face` in the page; one that fails to load, or a stack with no generic family and none of its fonts there, draws the fallback with one `console.warn`. The options are in [references/parameters.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.3.0/references/parameters.md#text-words-as-the-object); the Lab has them under Object → Words.

**Plain background** (studio white, paper, black velvet, a flat colour): key it out with `scripts/cutout.py`.

```bash
python3 scripts/cutout.py input.jpg out.png                        # background = median of the border
python3 scripts/cutout.py input.jpg out.png --bg "#efe6d2" --tolerance 0.14
python3 scripts/cutout.py input.jpg out.png --crop 120,40,980,1010 --keep-largest   # --keep-largest needs scipy
python3 scripts/cutout.py logo.jpg logo.png --no-flood                              # a logo with enclosed counters
```

It applies the photo's EXIF orientation, removes only background connected to the border, un-mixes the backdrop from edge pixels, crops, downsizes to ≤ 1024 px and feathers the outline. Always look at the output.

**A logo or wordmark on white:** the insides of O, B, e are enclosed by the letters, so the default keeps them opaque white. Use `--no-flood` (it keys every background-coloured pixel); cutout.py prints a note when it sees such enclosed areas.

- Background left as speckles or a rim: raise `--tolerance` in 0.02 steps, or pass `--bg` sampled from the backdrop.
- Parts of the subject eaten (pale fur, a white collar): lower `--tolerance` or `--softness`.
- Stray islands (dust, a signature): `--keep-largest`, or `--crop` them away first. Cast shadows count as subject: crop them or raise the tolerance.
- The border is not the background (a scan with a black edge, a frame): `--crop` to the paper first, because the background colour is read from the border.
- SVG needs no cut-out (pass it to silkify as it is); HEIC must be converted to JPEG or PNG first. cutout.py says so instead of failing with a traceback.

**Busy background** (a street, foliage, a room): colour keying will not work. Ask the user for a cut-out PNG or use a matting tool they already have (for example `rembg i in.jpg out.png`); do not install models without asking. Otherwise keep the rectangle (`cutout.py in.jpg out.png --rect`) and use `puff: 'cushion'`: a painting, poster or photo becomes a silk cushion, often better than a rough cut-out.

**Round things with a full texture** (the moon, a planet, a ball): `shape: 'sphere'` with a 2:1 equirectangular map; an optional elevation map (`bump`) adds relief near the terminator.

**A whole section as one sheet of silk** (a hero band, a backdrop): `fit: 'cover'` fills the canvas at any proportions, cropped like CSS `object-fit: cover`, and follows it when it is resized. Use it with a rectangle and almost no bulge: `{ image: 'cloth.jpg', fit: 'cover', matte: 'rect', corner: 0, puff: 0.05 }` on a canvas `position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%` inside the section. `examples/demos/fabric.html` is the whole page (with `breeze` and `breezeIdle`). A picture or words only: `fit: 'cover'` with `element` throws.

## Step 2: Pick the puff

The dome is built automatically when the picture loads. The only choice is how high it bulges:

| `puff` | Height | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| `'dome'` (1, the default) | a disc becomes a hemisphere | round objects: fruit, drops, pebbles, logos with a round body |
| `'cushion'` (0.6) | pillow | rectangles, paintings, cards, flat animals |
| `'flat'` (0.35) | a soft relief | wide wordmarks, thin or spiky silhouettes, portraits whose face must stay true |
| any number | | the height multiplier itself |

## Step 3: Pick a material and a feel

One word each. Anything you also write explicitly wins over the preset.

| `material` | Reads as, untouched |
|---|---|
| `'silk'` (default) | the photograph itself; silk only where touched |
| `'satin'` | a broad pale band of sheen across the threads |
| `'velvet'` | a dark body whose edges glow in its own colour |
| `'wet'` | a gloss spot; touched, sharp white glints |
| `'foil'` | metal: a body-coloured highlight made of streaks along the threads |
| `'paper'` | flat, warm, toothy, no gloss anywhere |
| `'ink'` | raking light, grain, the far side in hard shadow |

| `feel` | Moves like |
|---|---|
| `'silk'` (default) | medium waves that ring for a few seconds |
| `'water'` | small fast rings that run a long time |
| `'gel'` | a deep, slow dent that settles at once |
| `'jelly'` | big wobbles; holding pushes far in |

A dark picture (lacquer, a black logo) needs a brighter, broader highlight: add `sheen: 0.5, sheenPower: 14`. A painting or poster: `puff: 'cushion', floor: 0.6, sheen: 0.25`. Exact preset values and every other option are in [references/parameters.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.3.0/references/parameters.md). To find values by eye, open the [Lab](https://russguo.github.io/silkify/examples/lab.html), drop the picture in, and copy the options it prints.

## Step 4: Render it

The canvas sets the size: style it with CSS (for example `width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 1`). The page must be served over http(s), not opened as `file://` (the Lab's Download HTML is the exception: its picture is inside the file). A picture on another origin must send CORS headers.

On a phone, a finger that moves on the canvas would scroll the page instead of stroking the silk. `touch-action: pan-y` lets a sideways stroke ripple it while a vertical swipe still scrolls; `touch-action: none` makes the whole canvas strokable, at the price of the page not scrolling where the canvas is. Pick one; a canvas with neither can only be tapped.

```html
<canvas id="pear" style="width: 420px; aspect-ratio: 1; touch-action: pan-y"></canvas>

<script type="importmap">
  { "imports": { "three": "./node_modules/three/build/three.module.js" } }
</script>
<script type="module">
  import { silkify } from './silk/silk.js';      // src/ copied to ./silk/, three from `npm i three` (see Get the code)

  const pear = silkify(document.getElementById('pear'), { image: 'pear.png', material: 'satin' });
  await pear.ready;            // optional; pear.mode is then 'webgl', 'static' (drawn once) or 'failed' (see pear.reason)
</script>
```

The import map is required: silk.js imports `three` by name and hands the worker the URL the map resolves to.

**Bundlers (Vite, webpack, Next.js).** Copy `src/` into the project and `npm i three`, as in Get the code; never import the CDN URL from bundled code. silk.js starts its worker as `new Worker(new URL('./worker.js', import.meta.url), { type: 'module' })`, a form bundlers recognise, and the worker imports `three` itself, so both are bundled: no configuration, no `threeUrl`. `threeUrl` is only for a page without a bundler whose import map cannot be read (`worker: false` forces the main thread).

**The canvas belongs to the renderer.** In a worker the canvas is transferred and cannot be transferred again. On a remount, or when a failed worker falls back, silkify swaps in a fresh clone with the same attributes. Read the current element from `handle.canvas`, and never let a framework render that canvas: create it yourself, as below.

### React (and Next.js)

```jsx
'use client';                                           // Next.js app router; harmless elsewhere
import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import { silkify } from './silk/silk.js';               // src/ copied there; `npm i three`

export default function Silk({ src, options, className, style }) {
  const box = useRef(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');    // made here, not by React: silkify may transfer or swap it
    canvas.style.cssText = 'display:block;width:100%;height:100%';
    box.current.append(canvas);
    const silk = silkify(canvas, { image: src, ...options });
    return () => { silk.dispose(); silk.canvas.remove(); };
  }, [src]);                                            // options are read once; use silk.setTheme() to change them live
  return <div ref={box} className={className} style={{ aspectRatio: '1', touchAction: 'pan-y', ...style }} aria-hidden />;
}
```

Events are props in the repository's version of this component (`examples/react/src/Silk.jsx`: `onPress`, `onReady` and the rest, always calling the newest prop, so a re-render never remounts). A new `src` is a remount, and so are StrictMode's mount → unmount → mount and an unmount: React only owns the `<div>`, so it never removes a node it did not make. In Next.js a server component can render `<Silk>` directly: silk.js touches the DOM only inside the effect, so no `next/dynamic` is needed. Import a static asset as a URL string (`import pear from './pear.png'`, then `src={pear.src ?? pear}`). Checked with React 19.3, three 0.187.0, Vite 8.3 (dev and build) and Next.js 16.4 with Turbopack (dev and build): every mount is `'webgl'` on `'worker'`. The projects are `examples/react` and `examples/next`; `node scripts/frameworks.mjs` runs all four.

### The handle

| Member | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `ready` | Resolves to the handle once the first frame is on screen (live or static) or the mount has failed. It never rejects; check `mode`. It waits for the canvas to have a size: a canvas in `display: none` (a closed tab, a collapsed panel) resolves once it is shown, or at `dispose()`. |
| `mode` | `'loading'`, `'webgl'`, `'static'` (drawn once), `'failed'` (nothing could be drawn: the picture did not load or decode, or nothing in it is opaque) or `'disposed'`. A live object can turn `'static'` later: when the browser takes its WebGL context back (a background tab on a phone, a GPU reset, more contexts than it allows), it shows its poster or picture at once (a live element goes back to the page as plain HTML), and when the context is given back it rebuilds (`'loading'`, then `'webgl'` again); `onFail` and `onReady` tell the page (see Events). The canvas element is replaced on the way: read `handle.canvas`, do not keep the one you passed. |
| `reason` | Why it is `'static'` or `'failed'`, in words (`null` while live). A failure also logs one `console.warn` starting with `silkify:`, the library's only console output; so does code that could not be loaded (no import map for `three`, a file of `src/` not served), even when the object still ends up drawn once or on the main thread. A missing feature (no WebGL2, reduced motion) logs nothing. |
| `tier`, `thread`, `info` | The tier used (`'high'`, `'low'`, or `'static'`, also after a fallback to a still frame), `'worker'` or `'main'`, and what the renderer built: `{ tier, software, sim, steps, mesh, texture, ratioCap }` (`software`: a software rasteriser such as SwiftShader). |
| `setTheme(patch)` | Changes any material, light, ripple or motion option live (for example on a light/dark switch). `setTheme({ material: 'wet' })` swaps a preset. Scene options (`image`, `element`, `text`, `font`, `color`, `lineHeight`, `shape`, `puff`, `matte`, `corner`, `threshold`, `fit`, `angle`, `tier`, `worker`, `poster`) need a new `silkify()`: `setTheme()` ignores them without an error. Calls made before `ready` are kept and applied when the renderer starts. |
| `ripple(x, y, { depth, radius, hold })` | A programmatic touch at canvas coordinates (0–1, top-left origin): a short press, then the ripples run. Never counts as hovering; up to 4 at once; calls before `ready` are kept. The pointer is kept too: one still over the canvas when the renderer is up hovers (and presses) at once, and one that has already gone (a stroke across, a tap) gets one ripple where it first crossed the object. |
| `stats()` | A promise of `{ frames, mode, steps, touches }` (frames drawn, the loop's state, solver steps, touches the renderer made: `ripple()`, `autoplay`, a tap too quick for a frame), for measuring sleep. `mode` is `'active'`, `'breeze'`, `'tail'`, `'sweep'` or `'rest'` (no frames at all); live HTML adds `'html'`: the loop rests, but the element changed within the last second, and each change is a frame. Resolves at once when not live. |
| `setPhase(f)` | Sphere only: lunar phase 0–1 (0.5 full, `null` lit from the front). |
| `wake()`, `dispose()` | Restart a sleeping loop; remove listeners, observers and GPU resources (call it on unmount). After `dispose()`, `ready` and `stats()` settle with `mode: 'disposed'`. A second `silkify()` on the same canvas disposes the first. |

### Events

To react to a touch (count it, play a sound, vibrate) or to know how an object settled, pass callbacks in the options; they are options rather than DOM events because the canvas element can be replaced (above). x, y are canvas coordinates 0–1 from the top left, as `ripple()` takes them.

```js
silkify(canvas, {
  image: 'pear.png',
  onPress: ({ x, y, uv, pointerType }) => analytics.track('silk_press'),   // a pointer went down on the canvas
  onRelease: ({ over, held, uv }) => {},         // that press ended (over: lifted on the canvas)
  onStroke: ({ x, y, uv, dx, dy, pressed }) => {},   // moving on the canvas, at most every 100 ms
  onReady: (h) => report(h.mode, h.reason),      // 'webgl', or 'static' (a still frame); again after a lost context comes back
  onFail: (h) => report('fail', h.reason),       // 'failed', or a live object that lost its WebGL context ('static')
  haptics: true,                                 // one 10 ms vibration on a touch press, where the browser can (off by default)
});
```

`uv` is where the point lands on the object itself, `[u, v]` from the top left of the picture (the words' ink, a sphere's map as it is turned), or `null` off the object: it says which part was pressed whatever the `fit`. It comes from the renderer, so these three arrive a moment after the gesture, in order; while the renderer loads `uv` is `null`. A callback that throws is reported as an uncaught error and stops nothing else. Nothing is reported in the static tier or with `interactive: false`. Live HTML: a press on a link, a button or a field inside the element is that control's, not an `onPress`. The details are in [references/parameters.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.3.0/references/parameters.md#events-what-the-object-tells-the-page).

**The silk button.** When the object is something to press (add to basket, play, subscribe), make it a real `<button>` whose face is the silk, never a canvas with a click handler: it then takes focus, Enter and Space press it, and screen readers read it.

```html
<button class="silk-button" id="buy" type="button">
  <canvas aria-hidden="true" style="display: block; width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 1"></canvas>
  <span>Add a pomegranate</span>
</button>
<script type="module">
  import { silkify } from './silk/silk.js';      // and the import map for three, as in Step 4
  const button = document.getElementById('buy');
  const silk = silkify(button.querySelector('canvas'), { image: 'pomegranate.png', haptics: true });
  // mouse and touch dent the silk by themselves; the keyboard has no pointer, so press the middle
  button.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => { if ((e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') && !e.repeat) silk.ripple(0.5, 0.5, { hold: 160 }); });
  button.addEventListener('click', addToBasket);   // the action is the button's click, whatever pressed it
</script>
```

Give it a focus ring outside the canvas (`.silk-button:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid; outline-offset: 4px }`), pick a picture whose centre is the object (the keyboard's ripple lands there), and keep the action in `click`: `onPress` is for feedback only. So that the face is never empty while the renderer loads, put a poster `<img>` under the canvas and show the canvas only from `onReady` with `mode` `'webgl'` (hide it again on `onFail`, a lost context), as the gallery does; in the static tier (reduced motion, Save-Data) add no canvas at all, only the poster (`pickTier()` from `tier.js`). `examples/button.html` is the whole page, all of this included.

## Step 5 (optional): live HTML instead of a picture

When the thing to silkify is part of the page itself (a card, a quote, a sign-up box) rather than a picture, pass the element: `silkify(canvas, { element: '#card' })`. With HTML-in-Canvas (Chrome 157 and later) the element is printed on the silk and stays real HTML: its words can be selected, copied and found with the browser's search, links and buttons work, editable text takes typing (an input method too), it scrolls, and screen readers read it. In every other browser, and in the static tier, it simply stays where it is as plain HTML, with no warning: the page reads as it would without silkify.

```html
<div class="stage" style="position: relative; display: grid; place-items: center; min-height: 480px; touch-action: pan-y">
  <canvas id="silk" style="position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%"></canvas>
  <article id="card" style="position: relative; box-sizing: border-box; width: min(420px, 100% - 32px); padding: 24px; border-radius: 20px; background: #fbf7ef">…</article>
</div>
<script type="importmap">
  { "imports": { "three": "./node_modules/three/build/three.module.js" } }
</script>
<script type="module">
  import { silkify } from './silk/silk.js';      // as in Step 4: src/ copied to ./silk/, three from `npm i three`
  const silk = silkify(document.getElementById('silk'), { element: '#card' });
  await silk.ready;   // 'webgl': on the silk, once its first picture is there; 'static': plain HTML (silk.reason says why)
</script>
```

- **Lay it out for the fallback.** Put the element in the flow, centred in the canvas's box, with a width that also fits a phone (`width: min(420px, 100% - 32px)` and `box-sizing: border-box`, as above). Live, it is drawn 1:1 at that same place and size, and an invisible box of its size keeps its place in the page, so nothing moves when it comes alive, even when the element is what holds the stage open. An element larger than its canvas is shrunk to fit, with a margin, and silkify says so in one `console.warn`. An explicit `fit` scales it like a picture instead, without a warning.
- **Style the element by its own id or class.** It moves into the canvas while it is live, so `.stage > #card` would stop matching. Give it a background: its painted box, alpha included, is the silk's outline (its `border-radius` too), and transparent parts are cut away.
- **The canvas belongs to silkify.** No `user-select: none` on the canvas or its ancestors, or the words cannot be selected. An `aria-hidden` on the canvas would hide the element from screen readers: silkify removes it while the element is inside, and puts it back afterwards.
- **Call it once the element is rendered.** A selector that matches nothing, or `element: null` (a lookup or a React ref read too early), throws a `TypeError` at once. In React, call it in an effect.
- **Options.** `material`, `feel`, light and ripple options apply; `puff` defaults to `'cushion'`. Ignored without error: `matte`, `threshold`, `corner`, `albedo`, `grain`, `poster`. `image` together with `element`, or `shape: 'sphere'`, throws. The table is in [references/parameters.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.3.0/references/parameters.md#element-mode-live-html).
- **Cost.** Every change in the element's rendering (text, style, hover, selection, the caret blinking) is one snapshot and one frame; nothing changes, nothing is drawn. Keep continuous animation out of the element.
- **The handle.** `handle.element` is the element; `stats()` also reports `snapshots`, and `mode: 'html'` while they keep coming; `dispose()` puts the element back. If the browser has the feature but gives no picture of the element within 4 s, the element goes back as plain HTML: mode `'static'`, a reason, and one `console.warn`.

**React.** `examples/react/src/SilkHtml.jsx` (Next.js: `examples/next/components/SilkHtml.js`) renders its children inside a card `<div>`: silkify moves that `<div>` into its canvas while live and back on unmount, and React keeps rendering inside it. React never inserts or removes the card node itself, which is why the move is safe; style it through `cardStyle`. `scripts/frameworks.mjs` checks it under StrictMode in Vite and Next.js.

## Performance rules

- One live object per WebGL context, and browsers allow about 16. With more than three objects on a page, use `pool()` from `src/pool.js`, as the gallery and `examples/demos/logo-wall.html` do: a WebP poster in each element first, then `const wall = pool({ max: 3 }); wall.add(el, (canvas) => silkify(canvas, { …options, poster }))`. Each object mounts on the first `pointerenter` or `pointerdown`, is disposed when it scrolls away, and at most 3 are live; the pool prewarms (a worker with three.js) once the page is idle, so a mount on a touch only builds the scene (about 0.1 s instead of 1.2–1.4 s, measured in headless Chrome), and the touch that mounted it is answered. The API is in [references/parameters.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.3.0/references/parameters.md#exports).
- **The pool's elements need this CSS.** The pool prepends a bare `<canvas>` to the element and adds the class `live` once its first frame is up; the page sizes it and fades it in over the poster: `.tile { position: relative } .tile > img, .tile > canvas { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100% } .tile > canvas { opacity: 0; transition: opacity .25s } .tile.live > canvas { opacity: 1 }` (and `touch-action` on the tile, as in Step 4).
- **The poster is a frame of that canvas**, the object at rest at the size and `fit` it has live, so nothing moves when it comes alive. To make one: mount the object with `{ ...options, fillLight: false, idleSweep: false, interactive: false }`, and once it is up screenshot the canvas's box at 1× and 2× device pixels: in headless Chrome with a transparent default background (`Emulation.setDefaultBackgroundColorOverride`, as `scripts/shoot.mjs --posters` does for the gallery), or with Chrome DevTools' Capture node screenshot on a page whose background is the tile's. Save WebP next to the page (`posters/pear.webp`, `posters/pear@2x.webp`) and use the same file for the `<img>` and for `poster`. Without a rendered frame, show the picture itself laid out like the object (centred, `object-fit: contain`, at 1 / `fit` of the box: `width: 77%; height: 77%; margin: auto` for the default 1.3) and do not pass `poster`: the static tier then draws the picture the same way. A raw picture stretched over the whole tile is what makes the object shrink when it comes alive.
- The loop sleeps by itself: 60 fps while touched and for 8 s after, a 12 fps tail, then no frames at all. Only the pointer over the canvas counts: moving or holding it elsewhere on the page draws nothing. The waves step at a fixed 60 Hz, so they move at the same speed on a 120 Hz display. Phones and small screens get the low tier; reduced motion, Save-Data and no WebGL2 get a still frame (pass a `poster`: a URL, an `<img>`, a canvas, or a function `({ width, height }) => canvas` for a page that draws its own, as `fabric.html` does).
- `breeze` and `autoplay` keep the loop awake (breeze draws 30 fps while visible; `breezeIdle: 15000` stops it 15 s after the last touch until the next). Use them on one hero object, never on a grid.

Details and measurements: [references/performance.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.3.0/references/performance.md).

## Verify before you hand it over

1. Serve the page (`npx serve .`) and move the pointer across every object. Each should look like its material before it is touched, and ripple only where it is touched.
2. Check `handle.mode` is `'webgl'` and `handle.thread` is `'worker'` in the console (if not, `handle.reason` says why); the console must have no errors or warnings.
3. Test a narrow viewport (375 px, low tier) and `prefers-reduced-motion` (still frame).
4. Live HTML: in Chrome 157 or later, select a few words and copy them, click the links, and type into editable text; in Safari or Firefox the element must read as plain HTML.
5. If something looks wrong, look it up in [references/troubleshooting.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.3.0/references/troubleshooting.md): black square, halo, crease, no ripple, `thread` stuck on `'main'`.

In this repository, `node scripts/shoot.mjs` does steps 1–2 for every gallery card and the Lab in headless Chrome and exits non-zero on any failure; re-render posters with `--posters` whenever the look changes.
